WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Parser failure is part of normal operation.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:11.160
Input may be malformed, unsupported, incomplete, too large, out of sequence, or no longer relevant.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.420
The parser should be designed so those failures leave application state predictable.

4
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.220
Fail closed when required validation does not pass.

5
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:28.600
If the parser cannot prove the message satisfies the protocol contract, it should not produce a trusted object or trigger a state-changing action.

6
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.980
Continuing with guessed defaults makes the failure harder to reason about and harder to review.

7
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.180
Avoid building trusted objects from partially validated input.

8
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:45.000
It is safer to keep raw input and temporary decoded fields separate until the full message has passed the relevant checks.

9
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:50.780
Once validation succeeds, the parser can construct a trusted representation for later code.

10
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.980
Cleanup should release temporary allocations, buffers, and session resources after parse failure.

11
00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:04.120
That includes parser state, queued output, partially built objects, timers, and connection-specific resources.

12
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:10.060
Clean failure paths matter for long-running services because small leaks can become operational problems over time.

13
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:16.020
Error responses should provide useful protocol behavior without leaking sensitive internal details.

14
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:25.400
A peer may need to know that a message was rejected, but it usually does not need stack details, memory layout, private configuration, or authorization internals.

15
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.780
Repeated parse failures should also be handled consistently so the service remains reliable.
